Submission: Wir haben \varphi(N) = (p-1)(q-1) = N - p - q + 1, also \varphi(N) \approx N, mit einem Fehler in der Größenordnung \sqrt{N} (da p und q etwa gleichgroß sind). \textcolor{green}{Kommentar: Diese Identität ist korrekt. Die Abschätzung \varphi(N) \approx N mit absolutem Fehler \approx p+q \sim \sqrt{N} stimmt so.}

Außerdem ist ed = 1 + k\varphi(N) für ein k \in \mathbb{N} mit k < e. \textcolor{green}{Kommentar: Richtig. Für gcd(\varphi(N), e)=1 existiert eindeutig k im Bereich 0 \le k \le e-1, sodass ed = 1 + k\varphi(N).}

Daraus folgt d = (1 + k\varphi(N))/e für dieses k. \textcolor{green}{Kommentar: Dir richtig, da e | (1 + k\varphi(N)) und d ganzzahlig ist.}

Die führenden Bits von d können nun über die bereits erwähnte Approximation \varphi(N) \approx N ermittelt werden, da damit d \approx (1 + kN)/e ist. \textcolor{red}{Kommentar: Major flaw. Das Ersetzen \varphi(N) durch N ändert d auf absoluten Skalenebenen: \Delta d \approx k(p+q)/e, was ungefähr \Delta d \sim 2^{1008} (bei 1024-Bit-Parametern) ist. Die führenden Bits von d werden durch diese grobe Approximation NICHT zuverlässig bestimmt. Diese Annahme ist nicht legitim.}

Hierbei gibt es noch \approx 2^{16} Möglichkeiten für k. \textcolor{green}{Kommentar: Stimmt: Da k < e = 65537, gibt es ca. 2^{16} Kandidaten.}

Eve kann sämtliche Möglichkeiten durchprobieren und dasjenige k finden, das mit dem gegebenen Hash-Wert übereinstimmt. \textcolor{red}{Kommentar: Nicht gerechtfertigt. Ohne Kenntnis von \varphi(N) bzw. ohne Faktorisierung von N lässt sich d nicht zuverlässig aus k \;und\; N berechnen, und man kann den Hash der ersten 1000 Bits von d nicht sinnvoll gegen eine Testbabfrage verwenden, da d direkt von \varphi(N) abhängt. Die Annahme, man könne alle k durchprobieren und anhand des Hashes das richtige k finden, ist nicht gültig.}

Auf diese Weise lernt sie etwa die ersten 1024 Bits von d. \textcolor{red}{Kommentar: Falsch. Der Hash enthält 256 Bit; selbst theoretisch ist es nicht sinnvoll, aus einem Hash die ersten 1024 Bits von d sicher abzuleiten. Ohne exakte Kenntnis von \varphi(N) bleibt die Bestimmung der leading bits von d ungewiss; in der Praxis erhält man daraus keine 1024 Bits zuverlässig.}

Hinweis/Verbesserungsvorschläge:
- Realistische Einordnung: Ohne Faktorisieren von N (oder irgend eine Information, die \varphi(N) direkt fixiert) liefert der Hash der ersten 1000 Bits von d praktisch keine garantierte Information über die tatsächlichen Bits von d. Selbst wenn man 2^{16} mögliche k berücksichtigen würde, hängt d stark von \varphi(N) ab, das man nicht kennt.
- Eine korrekte Schlussfolgerung ist, dass man unter diesen Voraussetzungen theoretisch kaum Bits von d sicher ableiten kann; formal gesagt: aus dem Hash allein erhält man im Worst-Case keinen garantierten Informationsteil (0 Bits) über die 1000 führenden Bits von d. Informationstheoretisch könnte man höchstens eine sehr grobe obere Schranke angeben, aber kein konkretes positives Bit-Ergebnis wie “1024 Bits”.

Zusammenfassung:
- Realistische Kernaussage: Ohne Faktorisieren von N kann der Hash der ersten 1000 Bits von d nichts Sicheres über diese Bits liefern; der angegebene Weg führt zu einer zu optimistischen Behauptung (1024 Bits). 
- Richtig ist die Feststellung, dass k < e und damit ca. 2^{16} mögliche k existieren, aber die Brute-Force-Strategie über k allein reicht nicht, um d zuverlässig zu bestimmen, da \varphi(N) unbekannt ist. 
- Endergebnis der Aufgabe (0 Bits sicher learnbar) wäre die korrekte, konservative Antwort.

Here is an annotated version of the submission. Green comments are constructive clarifications, yellow notes flag minor ambiguities or notational quirks, and red notes flag real mathematical mistakes or gaps that should be fixed.

Submission (annotated)

- By Dirichlet's theorem on primes in arithmetic progressions, there are infinitely many primes both of the form $6k+1$ and $6k+5$. 
  \textcolor{green}{Comment: This relies on Dirichlet's theorem. If your course expects only elementary results, you should state this as an assumption or indicate an alternative approach.}

- Let $S_5$ and $S_7$ denote two such infinite sets. 
  \textcolor{yellow}{Comment: Be precise about the notation. Ideally, take $S_5=\{p \text{ prime}: p\equiv 5 \pmod{6}\}$ and $S_1=\{p \text{ prime}: p\equiv 1 \pmod{6}\}$ (or use $S_7$ to denote the same $p\equiv 1 \pmod{6}$ class, but that is nonstandard and confusing since $7\equiv 1\ (\mathrm{mod}\ 6)$. Consider renaming to avoid ambiguity.}

- Then $p + q$ with $p \in S_5$ and $q \in S_7$ is always divisible by 6, and there are infinitely many such pairs; this yields the desired result.
  \textcolor{red}{Comment: The conclusion that "there are infinitely many such pairs" does not automatically give infinitely many distinct sums. A safe fix is to fix one residue class for $q$ and let $p$ vary: pick any fixed $q_0\in S_7$, then for each $p\in S_5$ the sum $p+q_0$ is a multiple of 6 and all these sums are distinct (since $p$ varies and $q_0$ is fixed). This shows there are infinitely many multiples of 6 expressible as the sum of two primes.}

  \textcolor{green}{Alternate clear argument: Since $S_5$ is infinite, fix any $q_0\in S_7$. Then the numbers $p+q_0$ with $p\in S_5$ are all multiples of 6 and strictly increasing as $p$ increases, hence yield infinitely many such numbers.}

Additional small clarifications (optional but helpful):
- Note that for primes $p\in S_5$ (so $p\equiv 5\pmod{6}$) and $q\in S_7$ (so $q\equiv 1\pmod{6}$), we have $p+q\equiv 5+1\equiv 0\pmod{6}$, hence divisibility by 6. It is also true (as a bonus observation) that such sums are multiples of 3 and of 2, so they lie in $6\mathbb{N}$.
- If you keep the notation, consider using the standard pair of residue classes $p\equiv 5\ (\mathrm{mod}\ 6)$ and $q\equiv 1\ (\mathrm{mod}\ 6)$ to keep the congruence argument transparent.

Here is an annotated version of the submission. I’ve added brief notes in colored insertions to flag ideas, clarify steps, and point out minor clarifications.

- By Euclid's theorem on the infinitude of primes, there are infinitely many primes. \textcolor{green}{Comment: Correct opening; establishes there are infinitely many primes to work with.}

- By looking at divisibility by two and three, we can see that all primes greater than 3 are either 1 or 5 modulo 6. \textcolor{green}{Comment: Correct standard fact: primes > 3 are ≡ 1 or 5 (mod 6).}

- Therefore, there are \emph{either} infinitely many primes of the form $6k+1$ or infinitely many primes of the form $6k+5$. \textcolor{green}{Comment: Correct deduction: among primes > 3, at least one residue class mod 6 must be infinite because there are infinitely many primes.}

- Let $S$ denote an infinite set of such primes in either case. \textcolor{green}{Comment: For clarity, you may treat Case A (6k+1) and Case B (6k+5) separately; this sentence condenses both.}

- If the first case holds, then $p + 5$ is divisible by 6 for any $p \in S$. \textcolor{green}{Comment: If $p \equiv 1 \pmod{6}$, then $p+5 \equiv 0 \pmod{6}$; and since 5 is prime, $p+5$ is a sum of two primes.}

- If the second case holds, then $p + 7$ is divisible by 6 for any $p \in S$. \textcolor{green}{Comment: If $p \equiv 5 \pmod{6}$, then $p+7 \equiv 12 \equiv 0 \pmod{6}$; and 7 is also prime, so $p+7$ is a sum of two primes.}

- In either case, we have found infinitely many numbers that are divisible by 6 and that can be written as the sum of two prime numbers. \textcolor{green}{Comment: Good conclusion: you obtain an infinite family of multiples of 6 that are sums of two primes (p with either 5 or 7). The numbers $p+5$ or $p+7$ are all distinct as $p$ ranges over an infinite set.}

Question: Let N = pq be an RSA modulus, where p and q are distinct primes chosen uniformly at random from the set of all primes of size 1024 bits. Assume that the public key is (N, e), with e = 65537, and the private exponent is d. Eve wants to learn as much as possible about the private key d, but unfortunately her quantum computer is broken and she cannot factor N. However, she does know the SHA-256 hash value of the first 1000 binary digits of d, when these binary digits are interpreted as a binary number represented as a string. Determine how many bits of d she can learn from this information.

Submission: SHA-256 is a one-way hash function, and therefore Eve's knowledge of the hash value of the first 1000 binary digits of d does not reveal any practically useful information about d. Eve does have some information about d that is given by d obeying d ≡ e^{-1} mod φ(N), but this is not enough to learn any bits of d without knowing φ(N); and knowing φ(N) would allow her to probabilistically factor N. Therefore, unless N is of some special form, Eve cannot learn any bit-values of d from this information.

Your task is to create an annotated version of the student submission, where any mistakes or omissions of the student are highlighted as insertions in \textcolor{red} for real mistakes, \textcolor{yellow} for minor inaccuracies or clerical errors, and \textcolor{green} for other helpful comments. The goal of these comments is to help the student learn and to make it easy for a grader to scan the submission at a glance and decide how to grade it. Keep the comments short and clear.

Annotated feedback:

Question restatement and setup
- \textcolor{green}{Tip: The setup (1024-bit primes, N = pq, e = 65537) is standard for RSA; keep this context in mind when arguing about information leaks.}

Submission body

"SHA-256 is a one-way hash function, and therefore Eve's knowledge of the hash value of the first 1000 binary digits of d does not reveal any practically useful information about d."
- \textcolor{red}{Mistake: This is too strong. A 256-bit hash does not reveal the 1000-bit prefix exactly, but it does constrain it. Information-theoretically, the hash of a 1000-bit string can reduce the search space from 2^1000 to about 2^(1000-256) = 2^744 possibilities, i.e., it reveals up to ~256 bits of information about that 1000-bit prefix. Saying “does not reveal any practically useful information” ignores this information-theoretic bound.}
- \textcolor{yellow}{Clarification: The hash does not give you the 1000 bits, but it does reduce the uncertainty about that 1000-bit block by up to 256 bits in the information-theoretic sense. To answer the question, you should quantify this bound.}

"Eve does have some information about d that is given by d obeying d ≡ e^{-1} mod φ(N), but this is not enough to learn any bits of d without knowing φ(N); and knowing φ(N) would allow her to probabilistically factor N."
- \textcolor{green}{Helpful note: This is correct in spirit. Without φ(N), the congruence constraint is not enough to fix bits of d. If φ(N) were known, you could factor N and hence recover p and q, and then φ(N) exactly, which would let you compute d.}
- \textcolor{green}{Clarification: The phrase “probabilistically factor N” is a bit misleading; knowing φ(N) resolves p and q deterministically via the quadratic p+q = N - φ(N) + 1 and pq = N. So with φ(N) known, factoring N is straightforward.}

"Therefore, unless N is of some special form, Eve cannot learn any bit-values of d from this information."
- \textcolor{red}{Mistake: This overstates the conclusion. Even without φ(N), the hash of the first 1000 bits of d can restrict the possible values of those 1000 bits to a subset of size about 2^744, i.e., it conveys up to about 256 bits of information about that 1000-bit prefix. It does not allow you to deterministically learn specific bit-values, but it does provide an information-theoretic bound on how many bits of the 1000-bit prefix could be learned in principle. The answer should discuss an upper bound (≈256 bits) on information gained, not a blanket claim of zero bits learned.}
- \textcolor{yellow}{Clerical nuance: You could phrase the conclusion as "at most 256 bits of information about the first 1000 bits of d can be learned from the hash," rather than "cannot learn any bit-values."}

Overall assessment and answer guidance
- \textcolor{green}{Key takeaway to communicate: The SHA-256 hash of the 1000-bit prefix of d leaks at most 256 bits of information about that prefix. Thus, in terms of exact bit-values, Eve cannot deterministically learn any particular bit, but information-theoretically she can narrow the 1000-bit prefix down to about 2^744 possibilities (i.e., up to 256 bits of information). Without φ(N) knowledge, she cannot compute d; with φ(N) knowledge, she could factor N and recover the private key.}
- \textcolor{green}{Suggested final answer (precise): Eve can learn up to about 256 bits of information about the first 1000 bits of d (the most she can pin down is not a fixed set of bit-values, but a reduction of the search space from 2^1000 to about 2^744 possible prefixes). In particular, she cannot deterministically recover d without φ(N); and knowing φ(N) would let her factor N and compute d.}

Notes for grading
- The main correction needed is to move from “no bits can be learned” to “up to 256 bits of information about the 1000-bit prefix can be learned” due to the 256-bit SHA-256 output.
- The rest of the reasoning (consequence of φ(N) knowledge, factoring implications) is on the right track, but should be stated with the correct information-theoretic bound in mind.

Bottom line (recommended concise answer to the question)
- Eve can learn at most 256 bits of information about the first 1000 binary digits of d. Equivalently, the 1000-bit prefix of d can be constrained to about 2^744 possibilities, but no specific bit-values of d are guaranteed from the hash alone. Without φ(N), d remains undetermined; with φ(N), N can be factored and d recovered.
